## Why This Is Asked

This question probes ability to design low-latency, multilingual code-switch moderation in production, including privacy, safe rollout, and evaluation across EN/ES/code-switch slices. It also tests data flow, guardrails, and testing plans.

## Key Concepts

- Code-switch aware modeling
- Per-tenant privacy with embedding isolation
- Real-time streaming inference with sub-50 ms latency
- Canary rollout and rollback mechanisms
- Evaluation strategies across languages and code-switch slices

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-flow
const msg = receive();
const lang = identify(msg);
const features = extract(msg, lang);
const score = classify(features);
if (score.harassment) takeAction(score);
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you monitor drift in code-switch models?
- How would you handle lexicon-driven updates safely?

## Why This Is Asked

Assess ability to design real-time, privacy-preserving, multilingual NLP pipelines with regulatory constraints and governance.

## Key Concepts

- Real-time streaming architectures (Kafka, Kinesis)
- Privacy-preserving embeddings and federated updates
- NER + relation extraction for ADE
- Per-language drift detection and auditability

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo drift detector snippet
function klDivergence(p, q) {
  let sum=0;
  for (let i=0;i<p.length;i++){
    if (p[i]===0||q[i]===0) continue;
    sum += p[i]*Math.log(p[i]/q[i]);
  }
  return sum;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle multilingual tokenization for languages with scarce resources?
- What testing strategy ensures regulatory audit trails are verifiable?

## Why This Is Asked
Addresses real-time multilingual safety, reduces model hallucinations, and tight latency with on-device processing and privacy-preserving checks.

## Key Concepts
- Retrieval-Augmented Generation
- On-device inference
- External verifier for factuality
- Drift detection and explainability
- Auditable rollback

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-pipeline wiring
const stream = createLiveStream(source);
stream.map(msg => translateToEN(msg)).then(en => {
  const intent = classifyIntent(en);
  const knowledge = fetchKB(en, intent);
  const reply = generatePolicyResponse(knowledge, en);
  if (verifyWithSources(reply, knowledge)) emit(reply);
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you keep the private KB synchronized with policy updates without downtime?
- How would you quantify and meet the <180 ms latency target under load?

## Why This Is Asked

This angle tests end-to-end system thinking across multilingual NLP, streaming latency, privacy-by-design, RAG, and deployment.

## Key Concepts

- Multilingual intent classifier with adapters trained on English/Spanish/Japanese corpora
- Streaming ASR integration and latency budgets (target ~150 ms)
- Privacy: data minimization, on-device processing where possible, PII masking, tenant isolation
- Retrieval-Augmented Generation for consistent responses
- Deployment: feature flags, drift monitoring, rollback strategies

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code illustrating a multilingual intent classifier with adapters
const { Tokenizer, Model } = require('transformers-js');
const tokenizer = Tokenizer.fromPretrained('xlm-roberta-base');
const model = Model.fromPretrained('xlm-roberta-base', { numLabels: 50 });
model.addAdapter('multilingual');
model.setActiveAdapters(['multilingual']);
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you monitor model drift across languages and handle changes in intents?
- How would you design a safe fallback path when the classifier has low confidence?

## Why This Is Asked
The prompt tests ability to design privacy-preserving, low-latency NLP that handles multi-task outputs on-device, with hybrid architectures and secure updates.

## Key Concepts
- On-device inference for privacy
- Multi-task modeling (sentiment, intent, NER)
- Lightweight architectures and quantization
- Federated learning and secure cloud fallback
- Evaluation: macro-F1, latency, memory

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: on-device inference harness
function run(msg) {
  const tokens = tokenize(msg);
  const feats = extract(tokens);
  return model.predict(feats);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would code-switching affect on-device models?
- How would you validate latency across devices and OS versions?
